<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Bruce, 8th Earl of Elgin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1811">1811</a>–<a href="/years/1863">1863</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Lord Elgin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Bruce, 8th Earl of Elgin, was a British colonial administrator who served as governor-general of the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> from 1847 to 1854. By signing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rebellion-losses-bill">Rebellion Losses Bill</a> in 1849 despite riots that burned the parliament in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, he confirmed responsible government. He negotiated the Reciprocity Treaty of 1854 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and later served as viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-bruce-8th-earl-of-elgin.jpg" alt="James Bruce, 8th Earl of Elgin" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Felice Beato · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Felice_Beato_(British,_born_Italy_-_Portrait_of_Lord_Elgin,_Plenipotentiary_and_Ambassador,_Who_Signed_the_Treaty_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>James Bruce was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 20 July 1811, the son of the 7th Earl of Elgin and his second wife, Elizabeth Oswald. Bruce was educated at Eton College and Christ Church, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, graduating with a first in Classics in 1832, and became a friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ewart-gladstone">William Ewart Gladstone</a> there. He was elected a Member of Parliament for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southampton">Southampton</a> in 1841, but the election was declared void, and in November 1841 he succeeded his father as Earl of Elgin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elgin was Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> from 1842. In 1847 he became Governor General of the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, where the first real attempts at responsible government began. In 1849 the Baldwin-Lafontaine government passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rebellion-losses-bill">Rebellion Losses Bill</a>, compensating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-canadians">French Canadians</a> for losses in the rebellions of 1837. Elgin gave royal assent, and an English-speaking mob burned the Parliament Buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> and assaulted him. Rather than call in the military he withdrew his family to their country residence and let the civil authorities restore order. In 1854 he negotiated the Reciprocity Treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and gave assent to the law abolishing the seigneurial system in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, then resigned.</p><p>In 1857 he was appointed High Commissioner and Plenipotentiary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the Far East. He signed the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tientsin</a> on 26 June 1858 and in August 1858 the Treaty of Amity and Commerce with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. After Chinese troops attacked British positions in 1859 he returned in 1860 to demand an apology, the execution of the treaty and an indemnity. On 18 October 1860 he ordered the Old Summer Palace destroyed in retaliation for the torture and execution of almost twenty European and Indian prisoners, and on 24 October he signed the Convention of Peking, which ceded part of Kowloon Peninsula to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1862 Elgin became viceroy and governor-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the first viceroy appointed directly by the Crown. While travelling to Sialkot in 1863 he suffered a heart attack as he crossed a rope and wood bridge over the Chadly river, and he died on 20 November 1863. He was buried at St. John in the Wilderness in Dharamshala. Places named for him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> include Elgin County, Bruce County and the towns of Kincardine, Port Elgin and Bruce Mines, and the Bruce Peninsula.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James Bruce, 8th Earl of Elgin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Reformers Robert Baldwin and Louis-Hippolyte LaFontaine achieved responsible government in 1848, confirmed when <b>Lord Elgin</b> signed the Rebellion Losses Bill despite riots that burned Montreal's parliament in 1849.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-canada">Province of Canada</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Dharamshala, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1844" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of James Bruce, 8th Earl of Elgin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1844">What was here in 1844? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
